window.BioChecks[31] = {"2004-Q9":[{"q":"What does 'contractile' mean for cardiac muscle?","right":"It can shorten (contract)","wrong":["It can conduct electrical impulses","It can stretch and spring back","It works without ever tiring"],"why":"Contractile means able to contract: the muscle fibres shorten to squeeze blood out of the heart.","hint":"Break the word down: what does a muscle do to its length when it works?"},{"q":"Which heart chamber has the most muscle in its wall?","right":"Left ventricle","wrong":["Right ventricle","Left atrium","Right atrium"],"why":"The left ventricle pumps blood all round the body, so it needs the thickest, strongest wall.","hint":"Which chamber has to pump blood the furthest, all the way round the body?"},{"q":"Describe how you dissected the heart to see the atria and ventricles. (3 points)","right":"Left side is firmer; cut it open with a scalpel on a board","wrong":["Left side is softer; cut it open with a scalpel on a board","Right side is firmer; cut it open with a scalpel on a board","Left side is firmer; cut it open with forceps on a board"],"why":"Three of: tell left (firmer) from right, front side up, work on a board, use a scalpel, describe the cut, and a safety step.","hint":"Step by step: how you told the sides apart, what you cut on and with, where you cut, a safety step."},{"q":"On your diagram, where should the bicuspid valve go?","right":"Between left atrium and left ventricle","wrong":["Between right atrium and right ventricle","At the base of the aorta","At the base of the pulmonary artery"],"why":"The bicuspid valve is on the left side; the tricuspid is between the right atrium and right ventricle.","hint":"Show four chambers, with a valve between the upper and lower chamber on each side. Count the flaps."},{"q":"How do you expose a semilunar valve?","right":"Cut open the aorta (or pulmonary artery)","wrong":["Cut down through the septum","Cut open the left atrium","Cut open the vena cava"],"why":"Semilunar valves sit at the base of the aorta and pulmonary artery, so you cut into those vessels.","hint":"Semilunar valves guard the exits from the heart. Which vessels leave the ventricles?"},{"q":"What does a semilunar valve do?","right":"Stops blood flowing back into the ventricle","wrong":["Stops blood flowing back into the atrium","Lets blood pass from atrium to ventricle","Starts off each heartbeat"],"why":"When the ventricle relaxes, the semilunar valve snaps shut so blood in the artery can't fall back in.","hint":"All valves share one job. Without it, what would artery blood do when the ventricle relaxes?"},{"q":"Where does the coronary artery begin?","right":"At the base of the aorta","wrong":["At the base of the pulmonary artery","In the wall of the right atrium","Where the vena cava enters"],"why":"The coronary arteries branch off the aorta just above the semilunar valve, bringing oxygen-rich blood.","hint":"The heart feeds itself first: look where oxygen-rich blood leaves the left ventricle."}],"2005-Q3":[{"q":"True or false: the SA node (pacemaker) is on the right side of the heart.","right":"True","wrong":["False"],"why":"The pacemaker sits in the wall of the right atrium, near where the vena cava enters.","hint":"The pacemaker sits near where blood from the body first enters the heart. Which side is that?"},{"q":"True or false: human red blood cells have no nucleus.","right":"True","wrong":["False"],"why":"Red blood cells lose their nucleus as they mature, which leaves more room for haemoglobin.","hint":"Think how red cells make as much room as possible for haemoglobin."}],"2006-Q5":[{"q":"On the diagram, which blood vessel is Y?","right":"Hepatic portal vein","wrong":["Hepatic vein","Hepatic artery","Inferior vena cava"],"why":"Y links the intestine to the liver: that is the hepatic portal vein, carrying absorbed food.","hint":"X is the large organ beside the stomach. Y carries absorbed food from the gut straight to X."},{"q":"Which way does blood flow in Y, the hepatic portal vein?","right":"From the intestine into the liver","wrong":["From the liver into the intestine","From the liver towards the heart","From the heart into the liver"],"why":"It carries food absorbed in the gut to the liver, so its arrow points towards the liver.","hint":"Arteries bring blood to an organ and veins take it away. Where does Y pick up its food?"}],"2006-Q13a":[{"q":"Where in the body are red blood cells made?","right":"Bone marrow","wrong":["Liver","Spleen","Lymph nodes"],"why":"Red cells are made in the marrow of bones like the ribs, sternum and skull; liver and spleen break them down.","hint":"Think of the soft, spongy tissue found inside your ribs, sternum and skull."},{"q":"Give two ways red blood cells differ from cheek cells. (2 points)","right":"They have no nucleus and contain haemoglobin","wrong":["They have a nucleus and contain haemoglobin","They have no nucleus and have more mitochondria","They have no cell membrane and contain haemoglobin"],"why":"Two of: red cells have no nucleus, no mitochondria, contain haemoglobin, carry oxygen, and are smaller biconcave discs.","hint":"Think what a red cell has lost as it matures, and what it is packed with to carry oxygen."}],"2006-Q13b":[{"q":"Explain the terms plasma and glomerular filtrate. (2 points)","right":"Plasma: liquid part of blood; filtrate: plasma minus its proteins","wrong":["Plasma: blood cells and platelets; filtrate: plasma minus proteins","Plasma: liquid part of blood; filtrate: urine in the bladder","Plasma: liquid part of blood; filtrate: blood in the glomerulus"],"why":"Two points: plasma is the liquid part of blood; glomerular filtrate is plasma (minus its proteins) that has passed into Bowman's capsule.","hint":"Picture blood with its cells spun out. Then: what leaks into Bowman's capsule, and what can't?"}],"2006-Q13c":[{"q":"Which two parts make up the lymphatic system?","right":"Lymph vessels and lymph nodes","wrong":["Arteries and veins","Lymph vessels and venules","Lymph nodes and arterioles"],"why":"Lymph flows in lymph vessels, which pass through lymph nodes that filter it.","hint":"Think of the tubes lymph flows through, and the small swellings along them that filter it."},{"q":"Which is a function of the lymphatic system? (one of 6 points)","right":"Absorbs fats at the lacteals in villi","wrong":["Absorbs glucose at the lacteals in villi","Carries oxygen to the body cells","Makes new red blood cells"],"why":"Six points: three functions, each explained, e.g. returns tissue fluid to blood; absorbs fats at lacteals; nodes filter bacteria (defence).","hint":"Think where fluid leaked from capillaries goes, what villi absorb, and how germs are caught."}],"2007-Q13a":[{"q":"Which vessel brings blood back to the heart from the lungs?","right":"Pulmonary vein","wrong":["Pulmonary artery","Vena cava","Aorta"],"why":"Veins carry blood to the heart: the pulmonary vein brings oxygen-rich blood from the lungs.","hint":"Which type of vessel always carries blood towards the heart? Now find the one from the lungs."},{"q":"Name the main gas in the pulmonary vein's blood, and how it is carried. (2 points)","right":"Oxygen, joined to haemoglobin in red blood cells","wrong":["Carbon dioxide, joined to haemoglobin in red cells","Oxygen, as hydrogencarbonate in the plasma","Oxygen, carried inside white blood cells"],"why":"Two points: the gas is oxygen, and it is carried joined to haemoglobin (which contains iron), as oxyhaemoglobin in red cells.","hint":"This blood has just been through the lungs. What did it pick up, and which red pigment holds it?"}],"2009-Q13a":[{"q":"What are the two circuits of the circulatory system?","right":"Pulmonary and systemic","wrong":["Pulmonary and hepatic portal","Systemic and coronary","Arterial and venous"],"why":"The pulmonary circuit goes to the lungs and back; the systemic circuit goes to the rest of the body.","hint":"Blood does two loops from the heart: one just to the lungs, one round everything else. Name each."},{"q":"Which circuit does the left ventricle pump blood into?","right":"Systemic circuit","wrong":["Pulmonary circuit","Both circuits equally","Lymphatic circuit"],"why":"The left ventricle pumps blood into the aorta, which carries it round the body: the systemic circuit.","hint":"Left ventricle blood leaves through the aorta. Where does the aorta send it: lungs or body?"}],"2009-Q13b":[{"q":"Which best describes blood pressure?","right":"Force of blood pushing on artery walls","wrong":["Number of heartbeats in a minute","Stretching of an artery at each beat","How fast blood flows in the veins"],"why":"Blood pressure is the force blood exerts on vessel walls; the stretching of an artery is the pulse.","hint":"Pulse: what does each surge of blood do to an artery wall? Pressure: what does blood push against?"},{"q":"Which is an effect of a diet high in saturated fat?","right":"Fatty deposits narrow the arteries","wrong":["Arteries widen and get more elastic","Blood pressure goes down","Heart muscle gets stronger"],"why":"Saturated fat and cholesterol build up as plaque in artery walls, narrowing them and raising blood pressure.","hint":"Diet: think what fatty food does inside arteries. Exercise: think what it does to heart muscle."},{"q":"Give two ways, besides colour, red blood cells differ from cheek cells. (2 points)","right":"Full of haemoglobin and have no nucleus","wrong":["Full of haemoglobin and have two nuclei","Full of mitochondria and have no nucleus","Full of haemoglobin and have a cell wall"],"why":"Two of: red cells are full of haemoglobin, have no nucleus (or mitochondria), and are a biconcave disc shape.","hint":"Think what a red cell is missing compared with a cheek cell, what it's full of, and its shape."},{"q":"What do the SA and AV nodes do?","right":"Send impulses that make the heart contract","wrong":["Stop blood flowing backwards","Supply the heart muscle with blood","Keep the two sides' blood apart"],"why":"The nodes make and pass on electrical impulses: the SA node is the pacemaker that sets the heartbeat.","hint":"One of them is nicknamed the pacemaker. What does it send out, and what does that make muscle do?"},{"q":"Where precisely are the SA node and the AV node? (2 points)","right":"SA: right atrium wall; AV: septum near the tricuspid valve","wrong":["SA: left atrium wall; AV: septum near the tricuspid valve","SA: right atrium wall; AV: at the base of the aorta","SA: right ventricle wall; AV: left atrium wall"],"why":"Two points: the SA node is in the wall of the right atrium; the AV node is in the septum, near the tricuspid valve.","hint":"The SA node is near where body blood first enters the heart. The AV node is lower, by the valves."}],"2010-Q7":[{"q":"Which body cavity holds the heart and lungs?","right":"Thoracic (chest) cavity","wrong":["Abdominal cavity","Pelvic cavity","Cranial cavity"],"why":"The heart and lungs sit in the thorax, above the diaphragm; the abdomen is below it.","hint":"Which part of the trunk is above the diaphragm and protected by the ribs?"},{"q":"How does heart muscle differ from other muscles?","right":"It never tires (no fatigue)","wrong":["It tires very quickly","It is under voluntary control","It is attached to bones by tendons"],"why":"Cardiac muscle contracts all your life without fatigue, and it is involuntary.","hint":"Think how long your heart has beaten without a rest, compared with your arm muscles."},{"q":"How can you tell the front (ventral) side of the heart? (one of 4 points)","right":"Coronary artery runs diagonally across it","wrong":["Pulmonary veins enter on that side","It feels firmer when squeezed","No large vessels can be seen on it"],"why":"Four of: find the front, say how (coronary artery), name the scalpel, describe the first and second cuts, find a named structure, a safety step.","hint":"Go in order: find the front and say how, what you cut with, where the first and second cuts go."},{"q":"How do you expose a semilunar valve?","right":"Cut open the pulmonary artery (or aorta)","wrong":["Cut open the right atrium","Cut open the pulmonary vein","Cut along the septum"],"why":"Semilunar valves sit where the aorta and pulmonary artery leave the ventricles, so you cut into those.","hint":"Semilunar valves sit where blood leaves the ventricles. Which vessels would you open?"},{"q":"In your drawing, which chamber sits above the tricuspid valve? (one of 4 points)","right":"Right atrium","wrong":["Right ventricle","Left atrium","Left ventricle"],"why":"Four points: a drawing of the dissection, labelled right atrium, tricuspid valve and right ventricle. The right atrium sits above the valve.","hint":"Draw the right side opened up: an upper chamber, a lower chamber, and the valve flaps between them."}],"2010-Q15b":[{"q":"In your diagram, the hepatic portal vein links which organs?","right":"Small intestine to liver","wrong":["Liver to heart","Heart to liver","Liver to kidneys"],"why":"The hepatic portal vein runs from the capillaries of the small intestine to the liver.","hint":"Draw the gut and the large organ that processes food, with the vein linking them. Label all three."},{"q":"Which substance does the hepatic portal vein carry to the liver?","right":"Glucose","wrong":["Bile","Glycogen","Starch"],"why":"Glucose absorbed in the villi goes straight to the liver; starch must be digested first, and bile leaves the liver.","hint":"This vein drains the villi. Name any small digested food molecule they absorb."},{"q":"Which vessel brings oxygenated blood to the liver?","right":"Hepatic artery","wrong":["Hepatic portal vein","Hepatic vein","Pulmonary vein"],"why":"Arteries bring oxygen-rich blood to organs: the hepatic artery, a branch of the aorta, supplies the liver.","hint":"Which type of vessel brings oxygen-rich blood to any organ? Then give it the liver's name."}],"2011-Q8b":[{"q":"Why do heart and breathing rates rise during exercise? (2 points)","right":"More O2 and energy needed; more CO2 to remove","wrong":["More O2 and energy needed; less CO2 is made","Less O2 and energy needed; more CO2 to remove","Muscles make extra O2; more CO2 to remove"],"why":"Two points: muscles need more oxygen and energy, and make more CO2 to get rid of.","hint":"Think of what working muscles need more of, and what waste gas they make more of."}],"2011-Q12b":[{"q":"Which vessel supplies blood to a kidney?","right":"Renal artery","wrong":["Renal vein","Hepatic artery","Vena cava"],"why":"Arteries bring blood to organs: the renal artery, a branch of the aorta, supplies each kidney.","hint":"Arteries are named after the organ they feed. Then trace it back to the body's largest artery."}],"2012-Q7b":[{"q":"How did you expose the semilunar valves?","right":"Cut open the aorta or pulmonary artery","wrong":["Cut open the vena cava","Cut open the pulmonary veins","Cut across both atria"],"why":"The semilunar valves guard the openings of the aorta and pulmonary artery, so you cut into those.","hint":"Where do these valves sit? Open up the vessels that carry blood out of the ventricles."}],"2012-Q15b":[{"q":"Give two ways, other than colour, red cells differ from white cells. (2 points)","right":"Red cells have no nucleus and contain haemoglobin","wrong":["Red cells have a nucleus and contain haemoglobin","Red cells are larger and have no nucleus","Red cells have no nucleus and make antibodies"],"why":"Two of: red cells have no nucleus, contain haemoglobin, are smaller, are biconcave discs, and are far more numerous than white cells.","hint":"Compare what's inside each cell, their size and shape, and which is far more common."},{"q":"Which is a group of white blood cells other than lymphocytes?","right":"Phagocytes (e.g. monocytes)","wrong":["Platelets","Erythrocytes","B lymphocytes"],"why":"Phagocytes such as monocytes engulf germs; B cells are lymphocytes, and platelets aren't white cells.","hint":"Think of the white cells that engulf and eat germs, not the ones that make antibodies."}],"2014-Q3":[{"q":"On the diagram, what is part C?","right":"Lymph vessel","wrong":["Venule","Capillary","Arteriole"],"why":"C collects excess tissue fluid from around the body cells, so it is a lymph vessel.","hint":"C starts among the body cells and drains away extra tissue fluid. Which system is it part of?"},{"q":"What type of blood vessel is A?","right":"Arteriole","wrong":["Venule","Capillary","Lymph vessel"],"why":"A carries blood towards the capillaries, so it is an arteriole (a small artery); B drains them.","hint":"Does A carry blood into the capillaries or drain it away? Name that small vessel type."},{"q":"How would a section through A differ from one through B?","right":"A has a thicker wall","wrong":["A has a wider lumen","A has valves","A's wall is one cell thick"],"why":"A carries blood under higher pressure, so it has a thicker wall and narrower lumen than B.","hint":"A carries blood at higher pressure than B. How would its wall and lumen look different?"},{"q":"Which is a function of the lymphatic system? (one of 2 points)","right":"Returns excess tissue fluid to the blood","wrong":["Carries oxygen to the body cells","Makes new red blood cells","Removes urea from the blood"],"why":"Two of: returns tissue fluid to the blood, makes or matures lymphocytes, filters bacteria, fights infection, and transports things like fats.","hint":"Think where leaked tissue fluid ends up, and what the nodes do to germs."},{"q":"How does lymph differ from blood?","right":"Lymph has no red blood cells","wrong":["Lymph has no white blood cells","Lymph has less fat than blood","Lymph has more haemoglobin"],"why":"Red cells are too big to leave capillaries, so lymph has none; it does carry white cells (lymphocytes).","hint":"Lymph starts as fluid that leaked out of capillaries. Which blood cells are too big to get out?"},{"q":"Which major vessel returns the blood in B to the heart?","right":"Vena cava","wrong":["Aorta","Pulmonary vein","Pulmonary artery"],"why":"Blood from body tissues returns to the right atrium in the vena cava; pulmonary veins only drain the lungs.","hint":"B drains body tissue. Which large vein brings blood from the body into the heart?"}],"2014-Q9b":[{"q":"Where is the tricuspid valve?","right":"Between right atrium and right ventricle","wrong":["Between left atrium and left ventricle","At the base of the pulmonary artery","At the base of the aorta"],"why":"Tricuspid is on the right, between right atrium and right ventricle; the bicuspid is on the left.","hint":"It sits between an upper and a lower chamber. Is it on the side that pumps to the lungs or body?"},{"q":"How do you cut to expose the tricuspid valve?","right":"Cut open the right side with a scalpel","wrong":["Cut open the left side with a scalpel","Cut open the aorta with a scalpel","Cut across the septum with a scalpel"],"why":"The tricuspid valve is on the right side, so you open the right atrium and ventricle with a scalpel.","hint":"Which side of the heart is this valve on? Then think what tool you cut with and where."},{"q":"Where are the semilunar valves found?","right":"At the base of the aorta and pulmonary artery","wrong":["Between the atria and the ventricles","Where the venae cavae enter the atrium","Inside the pulmonary veins"],"why":"Semilunar valves sit where the aorta and pulmonary artery leave the ventricles.","hint":"Follow blood out of each ventricle. Where does it pass into the big arteries?"},{"q":"What did you measure first, before any exercise?","right":"The resting pulse (or breathing) rate","wrong":["The highest rate during exercise","The recovery time after exercise","The person's blood pressure"],"why":"You need the resting rate first so you can compare the rates after exercise with it.","hint":"Before exercise, you need a starting value to compare later readings with. What is it?"},{"q":"How did you measure pulse rate? (2 points)","right":"Feel the pulse at the wrist; count beats for 1 minute","wrong":["Feel the pulse in a wrist vein; count beats for 1 minute","Feel the wrist pulse; count for 1 minute, then divide by 60","Listen to the lungs; count the beats for 1 minute"],"why":"Two of: feel the pulse (at the wrist or neck), count the beats for a set time such as 1 minute, then repeat and take the average.","hint":"Where can you feel an artery near the skin? Then: count what, for how long, and how many times?"}],"2014-Q12c":[{"q":"Which capillary feature allows rapid uptake of CO2?","right":"Wall only one cell thick","wrong":["Thick muscular wall","Valves along its length","Very wide lumen"],"why":"A wall one cell thick gives a very short distance for gases to diffuse across.","hint":"Gases diffuse fastest over a short distance. What about the capillary wall makes it tiny?"},{"q":"Why is there more CO2 in venous blood than arterial blood? (2 points)","right":"Venous blood collects CO2 made by respiring body cells","wrong":["Venous blood collects CO2 from the air in the lungs","Body cells use up the CO2 carried in arterial blood","CO2 from the air soaks into veins through the skin"],"why":"Two points: venous blood has collected CO2 from respiring body cells, while arterial blood has had its CO2 cleared in the lungs.","hint":"Where has venous blood just been, and which process in those cells makes this gas?"},{"q":"Which vessel is an exception to this, and why?","right":"Pulmonary vein: CO2 removed in the lungs","wrong":["Renal vein: kidneys remove the CO2","Hepatic vein: liver removes the CO2","Vena cava: blood has been to the lungs"],"why":"Pulmonary veins carry blood just cleared of CO2 in the lungs (the pulmonary artery is the reverse case).","hint":"Think of vessels linked to the lungs, where blood loses this gas. Which vein leaves there?"}],"2015-Q4":[{"q":"True or false: mitosis happens in mature human red blood cells.","right":"False","wrong":["True"],"why":"Mature red blood cells have no nucleus, so they cannot divide by mitosis.","hint":"To divide by mitosis a cell must copy its DNA. Where is the DNA in a mature red cell?"}],"2016-Q6":[{"q":"Why can't mature red blood cells divide?","right":"They have no nucleus","wrong":["They have no cell membrane","Their nucleus is too small","They have no cytoplasm"],"why":"Cell division needs a nucleus with DNA to copy; mature red cells have lost theirs.","hint":"What does a cell need to copy before it can divide, and where is that kept?"}],"2016-Q8":[{"q":"What is the pulse?","right":"Rhythmic stretching of an artery wall","wrong":["Rhythmic stretching of a vein wall","Force of blood on the vessel walls","Sound of the heart valves closing"],"why":"Each heartbeat pushes a surge of blood into the arteries, stretching their walls: that is the pulse.","hint":"Each heartbeat sends a surge of blood into an artery. What does that do to its wall?"},{"q":"Why is the wrist a good place to feel the pulse?","right":"An artery lies close to the skin there","wrong":["A vein lies close to the skin there","The artery there has valves","Blood pressure is highest at the wrist"],"why":"At the wrist an artery runs near the surface, so you can feel it stretch with each beat.","hint":"What must be just under the skin for your fingers to feel each beat?"},{"q":"What was the control in this investigation?","right":"The rate measured at rest","wrong":["The rate at the peak of exercise","The time taken to recover","The rate of a very fit athlete"],"why":"The resting rate is the baseline with no exercise, so it acts as the control.","hint":"What reading did you take before the person did any exercise at all?"},{"q":"What is the control for?","right":"To compare the exercise results with","wrong":["To make the results more reliable","To measure the recovery time","To warm the person up first"],"why":"A control gives a baseline to compare with; repeating the test is what makes results reliable.","hint":"A control is a baseline. What do you do with the readings after exercise and the baseline?"},{"q":"On your graph, how should B's curve compare with A's?","right":"B's rate rises more than A's","wrong":["A's rate rises more than B's","Both rise by exactly the same amount","B's rate falls during exercise"],"why":"An unfit heart pumps less blood per beat, so B's rate has to rise more to supply her muscles.","hint":"x-axis: what changes over the test? y-axis: what you counted. Whose heart must work harder?"},{"q":"How would you measure recovery time? (3 points)","right":"Count pulse per minute right after exercise; time till it's resting","wrong":["Count pulse per minute right before exercise; time till it peaks","Count pulse per minute right after exercise; time the exercise itself","Count red cells right after exercise; time till they're back to normal"],"why":"Three of: start immediately after exercise, count the pulse (or breaths) per minute, and time how long it takes to get back to the resting rate.","hint":"Think when to start counting, what to count each minute, and when to stop the clock."}],"2016-Q15a":[{"q":"What should your drawings of a vein clearly show?","right":"Thin wall, wide lumen and valves","wrong":["Thick wall, narrow lumen, no valves","Wall one cell thick, no valves","Thick wall, wide lumen, no valves"],"why":"Veins carry blood at low pressure, so they have thin walls, wide lumens and valves (seen in the L.S.).","hint":"Veins carry low-pressure blood. Show the wall and lumen in T.S., and what stops backflow in L.S."},{"q":"Which vein has capillaries at both ends?","right":"Hepatic portal vein","wrong":["Hepatic vein","Pulmonary vein","Renal vein"],"why":"It starts in capillaries in the intestine and ends in capillaries in the liver.","hint":"Name veins after the organ they drain. For 5, think of the vein from gut capillaries to the liver."},{"q":"How is blood moved along the veins?","right":"Squeezed by muscles; valves stop backflow","wrong":["Valves contract to pump it along","Strong pulses from the heart push it","Vein walls squeeze it by peristalsis"],"why":"Skeletal muscles squeeze veins as they contract, and valves make sure blood only goes towards the heart.","hint":"Vein pressure is low, so what nearby squeezes them, and what keeps blood going one way?"}],"2017-Q1":[{"q":"Which metal is in haemoglobin?","right":"Iron","wrong":["Magnesium","Calcium","Copper"],"why":"Haemoglobin has iron at its centre, which binds oxygen; magnesium is the metal in chlorophyll.","hint":"It's the metal you lack in anaemia, found in red meat and spinach."}],"2018-Q11a":[{"q":"Name two substances, other than food and water, carried in blood. (2 points)","right":"Urea and oxygen","wrong":["Urea and glycogen","Oxygen and starch","Glucose and amino acids"],"why":"Two of: urea, hormones, antibodies, oxygen, CO2, plasma proteins, enzymes, clotting factors. Glucose and amino acids count as food.","hint":"Think of gases blood carries, wastes it takes to the kidneys, and chemical messengers."},{"q":"Where does each enter the blood: urea and oxygen? (2 points)","right":"Urea: at the liver; oxygen: at the lungs","wrong":["Urea: at the kidneys; oxygen: at the lungs","Urea: at the liver; oxygen: at the heart","Urea: at the bladder; oxygen: at body cells"],"why":"Two points: the liver makes urea from spare amino acids and puts it into the blood; oxygen enters the blood at the alveoli in the lungs.","hint":"For each one, which organ makes it or takes it in? Where are gases swapped, where are wastes made?"}],"2018-Q11b":[{"q":"In your heart drawing, which vessel leaves the right ventricle?","right":"Pulmonary artery","wrong":["Aorta","Vena cava","Pulmonary vein"],"why":"The right ventricle pumps blood to the lungs in the pulmonary artery; the aorta leaves the left ventricle.","hint":"Draw four chambers with the left ventricle wall thicker, plus the vessels entering and leaving."},{"q":"What is the role of the AV node? (one of 4 points)","right":"Passes the impulse on so ventricles contract","wrong":["Starts each heartbeat in the atria","Stops blood flowing back into the atria","Separates the left and right ventricles"],"why":"Four points: SA node is in the right atrium, sends impulses to make the atria contract; AV node is in the septum, passes them on to the ventricles.","hint":"Both nodes send electrical signals. Which chambers does each set off, and where is each placed?"},{"q":"What is measured when blood pressure is taken?","right":"Force of blood against the artery wall","wrong":["Number of heartbeats per minute","Amount of oxygen in the blood","Volume of blood pumped in each beat"],"why":"Blood pressure is the force blood exerts on artery walls, read as systolic over diastolic.","hint":"Think what blood does to the walls of an artery as the heart pumps it along."}],"2019-Q5":[{"q":"What word describes the shape of the two faces?","right":"Biconcave","wrong":["Biconvex","Convex","Spherical"],"why":"Both faces dip inwards (concave), giving a big surface area for taking up oxygen.","hint":"Both faces dip inwards like a dent. What's the word for a surface curving in, on two sides?"},{"q":"What lets red blood cells squeeze through narrow capillaries?","right":"They are flexible and can bend","wrong":["They have no cell membrane","They are rigid and pointed","They split into smaller cells"],"why":"Red cells are flexible, so they can fold and bend to squeeze through capillaries narrower than themselves.","hint":"Think how a soft ball differs from a marble when pushed through a narrow tube."},{"q":"Which molecule in red blood cells carries oxygen?","right":"Haemoglobin","wrong":["Myoglobin","Fibrinogen","Albumin"],"why":"Haemoglobin, with iron at its centre, picks up oxygen in the lungs and releases it in the tissues.","hint":"It's the red, iron-containing protein that fills these cells."},{"q":"Why do red blood cells only live about 120 days?","right":"They have no nucleus","wrong":["They have a large nucleus","Their cell wall breaks down","They are full of mitochondria"],"why":"With no nucleus (or organelles) they can't make new proteins to repair themselves or divide.","hint":"Cells repair themselves using instructions in their DNA. What has a mature red cell lost?"},{"q":"Where are red blood cells made, and where are they broken down?","right":"Made in marrow; broken down in liver","wrong":["Made in spleen; broken down in kidney","Made in marrow; broken down in kidney","Made in kidney; broken down in liver"],"why":"Bone marrow makes red cells; old ones are broken down in the liver and spleen.","hint":"Made: deep inside certain bones. Broken down: a large organ that also makes bile."},{"q":"Which transport fluid has no red blood cells?","right":"Lymph","wrong":["Arterial blood","Venous blood","Bile"],"why":"Lymph forms from fluid that leaks out of capillaries; red cells are too big to get out, so lymph has none.","hint":"It forms from fluid that leaks out of capillaries; red cells are too big to escape."}],"2019-Q6":[{"q":"Which vessel does the lung capillary branch from?","right":"Pulmonary artery","wrong":["Pulmonary vein","Aorta","Vena cava"],"why":"The pulmonary artery brings deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle and branches into lung capillaries.","hint":"Which vessel carries blood from the right ventricle into the lungs?"}],"2019-Q14b":[{"q":"Describe the sounds made during the cardiac cycle. (3 points)","right":"Lub: bicuspid and tricuspid shut; dub: semilunar valves shut","wrong":["Lub: semilunar valves shut; dub: bicuspid and tricuspid shut","Lub: bicuspid and tricuspid open; dub: semilunar valves open","Lub: the atria contract; dub: the ventricles contract"],"why":"Three points: two sounds, 'lub' and 'dub', made by valves closing: 'lub' as the bicuspid and tricuspid shut, 'dub' as the semilunar valves shut.","hint":"There are two sounds. For each, which set of valves is moving, and are they opening or closing?"}],"2019-Q15b":[{"q":"Which vessel carries amino acids to the liver?","right":"Hepatic portal vein","wrong":["Hepatic vein","Hepatic artery","Lacteal"],"why":"Amino acids enter blood capillaries in the villi, then the hepatic portal vein takes them to the liver.","hint":"Which vein carries blood from the villi of the small intestine straight to the liver?"}],"2020-Q9":[{"q":"Why is blood in the right side kept apart from the left?","right":"Stops oxygenated and deoxygenated blood mixing","wrong":["Stops blood flowing back into the atria","Lets blood pass from right side to left","Makes both sides pump at the same pressure"],"why":"Keeping them apart sends fully oxygenated blood to the body and lets each side pump at its own pressure.","hint":"Where has the blood on each side just come from: the body or the lungs? What's different about it?"},{"q":"What does diastole mean?","right":"Heart muscle relaxing","wrong":["Heart muscle contracting","Ventricles pumping blood out","Atria and ventricles contracting"],"why":"Diastole is the relaxing phase when the chambers fill; systole is when the muscle contracts.","hint":"If systole is the squeeze, what is the muscle doing while the chambers refill?"},{"q":"How did you tell the left side from the right?","right":"Left side is thicker and firmer","wrong":["Right side is thicker and firmer","Left side has the pulmonary artery","Left side has the vena cava"],"why":"The left ventricle's wall is much thicker, so that side feels firmer when you press it.","hint":"Which side pumps blood all round the body? How would its wall feel when you squeeze it?"},{"q":"Where did you see the coronary artery (groove)?","right":"On the front (ventral) surface","wrong":["Inside the left ventricle","Inside the right atrium","Around the pulmonary veins"],"why":"The coronary artery runs diagonally across the front of the heart, on its outer surface.","hint":"Which way up did you lay the heart to start dissecting? The groove was on that side."},{"q":"Where do you cut to find the bicuspid valve? (one of 4 points)","right":"Into the left side, atrium to ventricle","wrong":["Into the right side, atrium to ventricle","Into the aorta, just above the heart","Across the septum between ventricles"],"why":"Four of: a shallow cut, with a scalpel, on the left side, on a board (or a safety step), and the valve is between atrium and ventricle.","hint":"Which side is this valve on? Then say what you cut with, how deep, and between which chambers."},{"q":"Where is a semilunar valve, and how did you cut to find it? (2 points)","right":"At the base of the aorta; cut up into the aorta","wrong":["Between left atrium and ventricle; cut into the aorta","At the base of the aorta; cut across the septum","Where the vena cava enters; cut into the vena cava"],"why":"Two points: a semilunar valve is at the base of the aorta (or pulmonary artery), and you cut up into that vessel to see it.","hint":"Semilunar valves guard where blood leaves the ventricles. Which vessel would you cut up into?"}],"2021-Q14b":[{"q":"Which blood vessel carries digested nutrients from the gut?","right":"Hepatic portal vein","wrong":["Hepatic vein","Lacteal","Vena cava"],"why":"Nutrients pass into blood capillaries in the villi, which join to form the hepatic portal vein.","hint":"This vein links two capillary beds: those in the villi and those in a big organ. What's it called?"},{"q":"Which organ do digested nutrients go to first?","right":"Liver","wrong":["Heart","Kidneys","Pancreas"],"why":"The hepatic portal vein takes nutrients straight to the liver, which processes them before the heart.","hint":"Which large organ processes food, stores glycogen and makes bile?"}],"2022-Q17d":[{"q":"What is the period when the heart muscle is not contracting?","right":"Diastole","wrong":["Systole","Atrial systole","Ventricular systole"],"why":"Diastole is when heart muscle relaxes and the chambers fill; systole is the contracting phase.","hint":"Two terms: one for the squeeze, one for the relaxed, filling phase. Hook: S for squeeze."},{"q":"In which diagram are the ventricles contracting, and how do you know? (2 points)","right":"Z: the semilunar valves are open","wrong":["Z: the semilunar valves are closed","Y: the semilunar valves are open","Z: the bicuspid and tricuspid are open"],"why":"Two points: diagram Z, because its semilunar valves are open (and the AV valves shut) as blood is forced out into the arteries.","hint":"Look at the valves at the base of the arteries in each diagram. Which way is blood moving?"},{"q":"Where is the SA node?","right":"Wall of the right atrium","wrong":["Wall of the left atrium","Septum near the tricuspid valve","Wall of the right ventricle"],"why":"The SA node (pacemaker) is in the wall of the right atrium; the AV node is lower, in the septum.","hint":"The pacemaker sits in an upper chamber, near where blood from the body enters the heart."},{"q":"Into which circuit does the right ventricle pump blood?","right":"Pulmonary circuit","wrong":["Systemic circuit","Coronary circuit","Hepatic portal circuit"],"why":"The right ventricle pumps blood through the pulmonary artery to the lungs: the pulmonary circuit.","hint":"Where does the right ventricle send blood: to the lungs or round the body? Name that loop."},{"q":"What causes the two sounds of each heartbeat?","right":"Heart valves closing","wrong":["Heart valves opening","Blood rushing through the veins","The SA node firing"],"why":"'Lub' is the AV valves snapping shut and 'dub' is the semilunar valves snapping shut.","hint":"Which heart structures flap open and shut with each beat? Which movement makes the noise?"},{"q":"What does the coronary artery do?","right":"Supplies the heart muscle with blood","wrong":["Carries blood from the heart to lungs","Drains blood away from the heart muscle","Carries blood from the body to the heart"],"why":"Heart muscle needs its own oxygen and food; coronary arteries bring it in, coronary veins drain it.","hint":"The heart is a hard-working muscle too. Where does its own oxygen and food come from?"},{"q":"Which is an effect of smoking on the circulatory system?","right":"Raises blood pressure","wrong":["Lowers the heart rate","Makes arteries more elastic","Strengthens the heart muscle"],"why":"Nicotine narrows blood vessels and speeds up the heart, raising blood pressure.","hint":"Smoking: what does nicotine do to vessels and heart rate? Exercise: what does it do to the heart?"}],"2023-Q6":[{"q":"Why is the septum needed in the heart?","right":"Stops oxygen-rich and oxygen-poor blood mixing","wrong":["Stops blood flowing back into the atria","Lets blood pass from the right side to left","Supplies the heart wall with blood"],"why":"The septum is a wall keeping deoxygenated blood (right side) apart from oxygenated blood (left side).","hint":"Think where the blood in each side has just come from: the lungs or the body."}],"2023-Q10b":[{"q":"Which instrument did you use to make the incisions?","right":"Scalpel","wrong":["Forceps","Blunt probe","Mounted needle"],"why":"A scalpel has a sharp blade for cutting; forceps hold tissue and a probe is for exploring.","hint":"Which instrument has a very sharp, fine blade for cutting tissue?"},{"q":"How do the walls of the left and right ventricles differ?","right":"Left ventricle wall is thicker","wrong":["Right ventricle wall is thicker","Both are the same thickness","Only the left wall contains muscle"],"why":"The left ventricle pumps blood all round the body, so its muscular wall is much thicker than the right's.","hint":"Which ventricle pumps blood all round the body? How would its wall compare?"}],"2023-Q17c":[{"q":"Which vein carries blood lowest in metabolic wastes?","right":"Renal vein","wrong":["Hepatic vein","Hepatic portal vein","Vena cava"],"why":"The kidneys filter urea and other wastes out, so blood leaving them in the renal vein has the least.","hint":"Name each vein after the organ the blood has just left. For 3, which organ filters out wastes?"}],"2024-Q6":[{"q":"Which pairing of systole and diastole is right?","right":"Systole: contracting; diastole: relaxing","wrong":["Systole: relaxing; diastole: contracting","Systole: filling; diastole: emptying","Systole: atria only; diastole: ventricles"],"why":"In systole heart muscle contracts and pumps blood out; in diastole it relaxes and the heart fills.","hint":"Both describe the heart muscle. Which one is the squeeze and which the refill? Hook: S for squeeze."}],"2025-Q16b":[{"q":"On the diagram, what is structure A?","right":"Capillary","wrong":["Alveolus","Bronchiole","Arteriole"],"why":"A is the capillary carrying blood past the alveolus (B); its wall is one cell thick for fast diffusion.","hint":"A: the narrowest blood vessel. B: where inhaled air ends up. C: which cell carries oxygen?"}],"2025-Q16d":[{"q":"Name two organs of the lymphatic system. (2 points)","right":"Spleen and lymph nodes","wrong":["Spleen and pancreas","Liver and lymph nodes","Thyroid and tonsils"],"why":"Two of: lymph nodes, lymph vessels, spleen, thymus, tonsils, adenoids. The liver, pancreas and thyroid are not lymphatic organs.","hint":"Think of what swells in your neck when you're sick, and what's at the back of your throat."},{"q":"Give two functions of the lymphatic system, other than maturing lymphocytes. (2 points)","right":"Returns tissue fluid to the blood; fights infection","wrong":["Returns tissue fluid to the blood; makes red blood cells","Carries oxygen to the body cells; fights infection","Removes urea from the blood; carries fats from the gut"],"why":"Two of: returns excess tissue fluid to the blood, carries fats absorbed in the gut, and fights infection (lymph nodes filter out germs).","hint":"Think where leaked tissue fluid goes, what lacteals in villi carry, and how germs get trapped."}],"2025-Q17a":[{"q":"Which vessel carries blood highest in oxygen?","right":"Pulmonary vein","wrong":["Pulmonary artery","Vena cava","Hepatic portal vein"],"why":"Blood in the pulmonary vein has just picked up oxygen in the lungs, so it is the richest in oxygen.","hint":"One loop serves the lungs, one the body. Which vessel leaves the lungs? Which leaves the gut?"},{"q":"What does the septum do?","right":"Keeps left and right sides' blood apart","wrong":["Stops blood flowing back into the atria","Sets the pace of the heartbeat","Supplies blood to the heart muscle"],"why":"The septum is a wall keeping oxygenated blood (left) apart from deoxygenated blood (right).","hint":"SA node: what's its nickname, and what does it send? Septum: it's a wall, so what does it divide?"},{"q":"What is the period when heart muscle is contracting called?","right":"Systole","wrong":["Diastole","Peristalsis","Pulse"],"why":"Systole is the contracting phase; diastole is the relaxing phase when the chambers fill.","hint":"Its opposite, diastole, is when the chambers relax and fill. Hook: S for squeeze."},{"q":"Give two structural differences between arteries and veins. (2 points)","right":"Arteries: thick walls, no valves; veins: thin walls, valves","wrong":["Arteries: thin walls, no valves; veins: thick walls, valves","Arteries: thick walls, valves; veins: thin walls, no valves","Arteries: wide lumen, no valves; veins: narrow lumen, valves"],"why":"Two of: arteries have thicker walls, no valves and a narrower lumen; veins have thinner walls, valves and a wider lumen.","hint":"Arteries carry blood at high pressure, veins at low. Compare wall thickness, lumen and valves."}],"2026-Q7":[{"q":"Give the precise location and main function of the SA node. (2 points)","right":"Wall of the right atrium; it is the pacemaker","wrong":["Wall of the left atrium; it is the pacemaker","Wall of the right atrium; it stops blood flowing back","Wall of the right ventricle; it is the pacemaker"],"why":"Two points: the SA node is in the wall of the right atrium, and it is the pacemaker, sending impulses that make the atria contract.","hint":"Find the upper chamber where body blood enters. What does this node send out, and what does it set?"}],"2026-Q16b":[{"q":"Why is a red blood cell a poor host for viruses?","right":"No nucleus or organelles to copy the virus","wrong":["It has a tough cell wall","It is too small for a virus to enter","Haemoglobin destroys viruses inside it"],"why":"Viruses need the host's nucleus and organelles (like ribosomes) to make copies; mature red cells have none.","hint":"A virus hijacks a cell's machinery to copy itself. What does a mature red cell lack?"}],"2026-Q17a":[{"q":"What type of vessel are X and Y?","right":"X: arteriole; Y: venule","wrong":["X: venule; Y: arteriole","X: artery; Y: vein","X: capillary; Y: lymph vessel"],"why":"X, the arteriole, brings blood from an artery into the capillaries; Y, the venule, drains them to a vein.","hint":"One feeds the capillaries from an artery, the other drains them to a vein. Name the small ones."},{"q":"Which best describes the pulse?","right":"Rhythmic expansion of the arteries","wrong":["Rhythmic expansion of the veins","Force of blood on the vessel walls","Number of breaths per minute"],"why":"Each heartbeat sends a surge of blood that stretches artery walls; the force on the walls is blood pressure.","hint":"Pressure: what does blood push against? Pulse: what happens to an artery wall at each heartbeat?"},{"q":"How does blood pressure change in the direction shown?","right":"It decreases","wrong":["It increases","It stays the same","It rises, then falls"],"why":"Pressure falls steadily as blood moves from the arteriole through the capillaries to the venule.","hint":"Blood is moving away from the heart through more and more branches. Does its push get stronger?"},{"q":"In your drawings, what should the artery show?","right":"Thick muscular wall and small lumen","wrong":["Thin wall and large lumen","Thick wall with valves inside","Wall only one cell thick"],"why":"Arteries need thick, muscular, elastic walls to withstand high pressure; veins are thin with valves.","hint":"Compare wall thickness and lumen size, show valves in the vein, and how thin the capillary wall is."},{"q":"Which vessel in the capillary network soaks up excess tissue fluid?","right":"Lymph vessel","wrong":["Venule","Arteriole","Vein"],"why":"Lymph vessels pick up the extra tissue fluid around the cells and return it to the blood as lymph.","hint":"These blind-ended tubes collect tissue fluid and return it to the blood. Which system are they in?"},{"q":"Which is an effect of regular exercise?","right":"Stronger heart muscle","wrong":["Higher resting heart rate","Narrower, stiffer arteries","Higher resting blood pressure"],"why":"Exercise strengthens the heart, so it pumps more per beat and the resting heart rate falls.","hint":"Exercise: what happens to trained heart muscle? Diet: what does saturated fat do to arteries?"}],"S1-Q3c":[{"q":"What is the main way CO2 is carried in the blood?","right":"As hydrogencarbonate ions in plasma","wrong":["As oxyhaemoglobin in red cells","Inside white blood cells","As gas bubbles in the plasma"],"why":"Most CO2 reacts with water to form hydrogencarbonate (HCO3-) ions, which travel in the plasma.","hint":"Most of this gas reacts with water first. Which ion does that form, and where does it travel?"},{"q":"What is the main way oxygen is carried in the blood?","right":"As oxyhaemoglobin in red blood cells","wrong":["As hydrogencarbonate ions in plasma","Mostly dissolved in the plasma","Inside white blood cells"],"why":"Oxygen binds to haemoglobin in red cells to form oxyhaemoglobin; very little dissolves in plasma.","hint":"Which iron-containing pigment picks up this gas, and in which cells is it found?"},{"q":"What goes in the table for arterial and venous pulmonary blood? (2 points)","right":"Arterial: CO2 high, O2 low; venous: CO2 low, O2 high","wrong":["Arterial: CO2 low, O2 high; venous: CO2 high, O2 low","Arterial: CO2 high, O2 high; venous: CO2 low, O2 low","Arterial: CO2 high, O2 low; venous: CO2 high, O2 low"],"why":"Two points: pulmonary artery blood, going to the lungs, is high in CO2 and low in O2; pulmonary vein blood, coming back, is the reverse.","hint":"Pulmonary artery blood is heading to the lungs; pulmonary vein blood is coming back. What changed?"}],"S1-Q5d":[{"q":"What are the two common blood grouping systems?","right":"ABO and Rhesus","wrong":["Type A and type B","Antigen and antibody","Positive and negative"],"why":"The ABO system (A, B, AB, O) and the Rhesus system (positive or negative) are the two main ones.","hint":"Think of the letters on a blood bag, then the plus or minus sign after them."},{"q":"Why is it important to know a person's blood group?","right":"So transfused blood is compatible","wrong":["To work out their heart rate","To choose which antibiotic to give","To find out if they are anaemic"],"why":"Wrong-group blood makes the recipient's antibodies clump the donor red cells, which can be fatal.","hint":"Think about giving blood to a patient, and a mother whose baby's blood differs from hers."}],"S2-Q5a":[{"q":"On the diagram, which vessel is B?","right":"Pulmonary artery","wrong":["Aorta","Pulmonary vein","Vena cava"],"why":"B is the pulmonary artery, taking blood to the lungs; A is the aorta, taking blood to the body.","hint":"Follow each from its ventricle: one arches over to the body, the other heads to the lungs."},{"q":"What happens to the labelled valve when the ventricles contract?","right":"It is pushed open","wrong":["It snaps shut","It stays closed","It opens into the ventricle"],"why":"Blood forced out of the ventricle pushes the semilunar valve open; it shuts when the ventricle relaxes.","hint":"Blood is being forced out of the ventricle into the artery. What must the valve do to let it?"},{"q":"Where should X (the pacemaker) go on the diagram?","right":"In the wall of the right atrium","wrong":["In the wall of the left atrium","In the septum between the ventricles","At the base of the aorta"],"why":"The pacemaker (SA node) is in the upper wall of the right atrium, near where the vena cava enters.","hint":"Which upper chamber does blood from the body flow into first? Look in its wall."},{"q":"How does plasma CO2 change between A and B, and why? (2 points)","right":"Higher in B: respiring cells added CO2; A lost CO2 in the lungs","wrong":["Higher in A: respiring cells added CO2; B lost CO2 in the lungs","Higher in B: it took in CO2 from the air in the lungs","The same in both: CO2 stays in the red cells all the way"],"why":"Two points: CO2 is higher in B (pulmonary artery), as respiring body cells added it; A (aorta) carries blood that lost CO2 in the lungs.","hint":"Where has the blood in each vessel just been: the body tissues or the lungs?"},{"q":"Which is a structural difference between an artery and a vein?","right":"Veins have valves; arteries do not","wrong":["Arteries have valves; veins do not","Veins have thicker walls than arteries","Arteries have the wider lumen"],"why":"Blood in veins is at low pressure, so valves stop it flowing backwards; arteries have thick walls instead.","hint":"Arteries carry blood at high pressure, veins at low. How would wall, lumen or backflow differ?"}],"S2-Q5b":[{"q":"What are the two circuits called?","right":"Pulmonary and systemic","wrong":["Pulmonary and coronary","Systemic and hepatic portal","Arterial and venous"],"why":"The pulmonary circuit takes blood to the lungs; the systemic circuit takes it round the rest of the body.","hint":"Blood leaves the heart on two loops: one only to the lungs, one round the rest of the body."},{"q":"Describe the pathway of blood in the pulmonary circuit.","right":"Right ventricle, pulmonary artery, lungs, pulmonary vein, left atrium","wrong":["Right ventricle, pulmonary vein, lungs, pulmonary artery, left atrium","Left ventricle, pulmonary artery, lungs, pulmonary vein, right atrium","Right atrium, pulmonary artery, lungs, pulmonary vein, left ventricle"],"why":"Name the circuit and its structures in order: right ventricle, pulmonary artery, lungs, pulmonary vein, left atrium.","hint":"Pick a loop and start at the ventricle that pumps into it. Then which artery, organ, vein, chamber?"},{"q":"What could happen to the heart if a coronary artery were fully blocked? (2 points)","right":"Heart muscle gets no oxygen and dies","wrong":["Heart muscle gets extra oxygen and grows","The heart beats faster and more strongly","Blood bypasses the lungs and the heart rests"],"why":"Two of: the muscle it supplies gets no blood or oxygen, so those cells can't respire and die, which is a heart attack.","hint":"What does this artery normally bring to heart muscle? What happens to cells cut off from it?"},{"q":"What is blood pressure?","right":"Force of blood on the vessel walls","wrong":["Number of heartbeats per minute","Speed of blood through the veins","Amount of blood in the body"],"why":"Blood pressure is the push of blood on artery walls; beats per minute is the heart (or pulse) rate.","hint":"As the heart pumps, what does the blood push against inside each artery?"}],"S2-Q5c":[{"q":"On the graph, what causes each steep rise in pressure?","right":"Left ventricle contracting","wrong":["Left ventricle relaxing","Semilunar valves closing","Left atrium contracting"],"why":"Each steep rise is the left ventricle contracting and forcing blood into the arteries: put X there.","hint":"When the ventricle squeezes, arterial pressure shoots up. Find the steepest climb on the graph."},{"q":"From the graph, work out the heart rate, showing your working. (2 points)","right":"One beat takes 0.5 s, so 60 / 0.5 = 120 bpm","wrong":["One beat takes 0.5 s, so 60 x 0.5 = 30 bpm","One beat takes 1 s, so 60 / 1 = 60 bpm","One beat takes 0.25 s, so 60 / 0.25 = 240 bpm"],"why":"Two points: one beat (peak to peak) takes 0.5 s, then divide 60 seconds by it: 60 / 0.5 = 120 beats per minute.","hint":"Find the time from one peak to the next. How many of those fit into 60 seconds?"},{"q":"Is the athlete at rest?","right":"No: 120 bpm is too high for rest","wrong":["Yes: 120 bpm is a normal resting rate","Yes: athletes always have fast hearts","No: athletes rest at over 150 bpm"],"why":"A resting rate is about 60-80 bpm (lower in a fit athlete), so 120 bpm means the athlete is exercising.","hint":"Compare your answer to (ii) with a typical resting heart rate. Is it higher or lower?"}],"S2-Q5d":[{"q":"Why do cardiac muscle cells have so many mitochondria? (2 points)","right":"Heart never rests so needs lots of ATP, made by aerobic respiration","wrong":["Heart never rests so needs lots of ATP, made by anaerobic respiration","Heart rests between beats so needs little ATP from respiration","Heart needs a big oxygen store, which mitochondria hold for it"],"why":"Two points: heart muscle contracts nonstop and needs lots of energy (ATP); mitochondria release it by aerobic respiration, so it does not tire.","hint":"How often does heart muscle rest? What do mitochondria make, and by which type of respiration?"}]};
